<SubSection title="Bevel Gears" id="Bevel Gears">
<p>Bevel gears are based on rolling cones rather than rolling cylinders, meaning they transmit motion between intersecting shafts (typically at <InlineEquation equation="90^\\circ" />). Because of this geometry, the pitch diameter varies along the tooth, and an average pitch diameter is used for analysis.</p>

    <Image src='/mf/Gears/Bevel Gear Diagram (1).png' width='7'></Image>

<p>The geometry is defined by pitch cone angles <InlineEquation equation="\\gamma_p" /> (pinion) and <InlineEquation equation="\\gamma_g" /> (gear), which satisfy:</p>

<DisplayEquation equation="\\gamma_p + \\gamma_g = 90^\\circ" title="" />

<p>The speed (gear) ratio can be expressed as:</p>

<DisplayEquation equation="\\frac{\\omega_p}{\\omega_g} = \\frac{N_g}{N_p} = \\tan \\gamma_g" title="" />

<p>An average pitch diameter is used for velocity calculations:</p>

<DisplayEquation equation="d_{av} = d - b \\sin \\gamma" title="" />

<p>The contact force acts along the line of action and can be resolved into tangential and normal components:</p>

<DisplayEquation equation="F_t = F \\cos \\phi" title="" />


<DisplayEquation equation="F_n = F \\sin \\phi = F_t \\tan \\phi" title="" />

<p>The normal force further resolves into radial and axial components:</p>

<DisplayEquation equation="F_r = F_n \\cos \\gamma = F_t \\tan \\phi \\cos \\gamma" title="" />


<DisplayEquation equation="F_a = F_n \\sin \\gamma = F_t \\tan \\phi \\sin \\gamma" title="" />

